TI  - Pulmonary hypertension with a low cardiac index requires a higher PaO(2) level to 
      avoid tissue hypoxia.
PG  - 97-103
LID - 10.1111/resp.13574 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND AND OBJECTIVE: The optimal oxygen supplementation needed to avoid 
      tissue hypoxia in patients with pulmonary hypertension (PH) remains unclear. This 
      study aimed to identify the arterial oxygen tension (PaO(2) ) level needed to 
      avoid tissue hypoxia which results in a poor prognosis in patients with PH. 
      METHODS: We retrospectively analysed the data for 1571 right heart 
      catheterizations in patients suspected of having PH between 1983 and 2017 at our 
      institution. Examinations were classified according to mean pulmonary arterial 
      pressure (mPAP), cardiac index (CI) and the presence of lung disease, pulmonary 
      arterial hypertension (PAH) or chronic thromboembolic PH (CTEPH). The PaO(2) 
      levels needed to avoid tissue hypoxia were compared in each subgroup. RESULTS: 
      The estimated PaO(2) equivalent to a mixed venous oxygen tension (PvO(2) ) of 35 
      mm Hg (tissue hypoxia) was 63.2 mm Hg in all patients, 77.0 mm Hg in those with 
      decreased CI (<2.5 L/min/m(2) ) and 57.0 mm Hg in those with preserved CI. 
      Multivariate regression analysis identified mPAP, CI and PaO(2) to be independent 
      predictors of extremely low PvO(2) . Similar results were observed regardless of 
      the severity of PH or the presence of lung disease, PAH or CTEPH. The PaO(2) 
      level needed to avoid tissue hypoxia was higher in patients with mild PH and 
      decreased CI than in those with severe PH and preserved CI (70.2 vs 61.5 mm Hg). 
      CONCLUSION: These findings indicate that a decreased CI rather than increased 
      mPAP induces tissue hypoxia in PH. Patients with PH and decreased CI may need 
      adjustment of oxygen therapy at higher PaO(2) levels compared with patients with 
      preserved CI.
