TI  - Molecular polymerase chain reaction diagnosis of pulmonary mucormycosis caused by 
      Cunninghamella bertholletiae.
PG  - 397-401
AB  - OBJECTIVE: Mucormycosis is an uncommon but frequently fatal infection caused by 
      strains of mucorales in immunocompromised hosts. In this study, we report a case 
      of pulmonary mucormycosis associated with acute lymphocytic leukaemia caused by 
      Cunninghamella bertholletiae, a rare pathogen. METHODOLOGY: Retrospective 
      analysis of the stored serum, sputum, and necropsy lung tissue samples from this 
      patient, enabled subspecies identification by means of panfungal polymerase chain 
      reaction (PCR), direct DNA sequencing of the PCR products, and homology search 
      with nucleotide basic local alignment search tool. RESULTS: The development of a 
      reliable diagnostic blood test for angio-invasive fungal infections such as 
      mucormycosis is desirable, because the sensitivity of culture for these fungi is 
      extremely low. CONCLUSION: Panfungal PCR on serial serum samples might be useful 
      for the diagnosis of pulmonary mucormycosis.
