DP  - 2015 May
TI  - Early pulmonary inflammation and lung damage in children with cystic fibrosis.
PG  - 569-78
LID - 10.1111/resp.12521 [doi]
AB  - Individuals with cystic fibrosis (CF) suffer progressive airway inflammation, 
      infection and lung damage. Airway inflammation and infection are present from 
      early in life, often before children are symptomatic. CF gene mutations cause 
      changes in the CF transmembrane regulator protein that result in an aberrant 
      airway microenvironment including airway surface liquid (ASL) dehydration, 
      reduced ASL acidity, altered airway mucin and a dysregulated inflammatory 
      response. This review discusses how an altered microenvironment drives CF lung 
      disease before overt airway infection, the response of the CF airway to early 
      infection, and methods to prevent inflammation and early lung disease.
