TI  - Comparison of performance status with peak oxygen consumption in operable 
      patients with non-small-cell lung cancer.
PG  - 105-8
LID - 10.1111/resp.12162 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND AND OBJECTIVE: In this era of increasing options for treatment of 
      'surgical' lung cancer patients, preoperative physiologic assessment of accurate 
      patient selection is becoming more important. The variability in an objective 
      measure of cardiorespiratory fitness (peak oxygen consumption (VO2peak )) across 
      performance in operable non-small-cell lung cancer (NSCLC) patients enrolled in 
      the Cancer and Leukemia Group B trial was compared. METHODS: Using a 
      cross-sectional design, 392 NSCLC patients underwent an incremental 
      cardiopulmonary cycling exercise test to symptom limitation with expired gas 
      analysis to determine VO2peak . Performance status (PS) was assessed using the 
      Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) tool. RESULTS: There was a significant 
      decrease in VO2peak across increasing ECOG categories (P < 0.0001). However, 
      there was a large range in VO2peak for any given ECOG category with overlap 
      between categories (ECOG 0: 5.0-31.5 mL/kg/min; ECOG 1: 4.3-24.8 mL/kg/min; ECOG 
      2: 8.9-21.9 mL/kg/min; ECOG 3; 3.3-11.7 mL/kg/min). CONCLUSIONS: PS scoring 
      systems do not provide a sensitive measure of functional status. Objective 
      measures such as VO2peak may be a useful in the clinical management of oncology 
      patients.
