TI  - The six-minute walk test using forehead oximetry is reliable in the assessment of 
      scleroderma lung disease.
PG  - 647-52
LID - 10.1111/j.1440-1843.2012.02133.x [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND AND OBJECTIVE: The six-minute walk test (6MWT) is a validated field 
      test in the assessment of interstitial lung disease but may not be so useful in 
      scleroderma (SSc) lung disease. The aim of this study was to determine the 
      reliability of the 6MWT in patients with SSc and correlate results with 
      morphological and functional measures of disease severity. METHODS: Thirty 
      patients (24 female, mean age 47, mean diffusing capacity of carbon monoxide 65%, 
      vital capacity 77% predicted) with American College of Rheumatology 
      classification of SSc performed two 6MWT using various oximetry sites, 1 week 
      apart, and underwent SSc-specific disease severity and quality-of-life 
      measurements, lung function, high-resolution computed tomography and 
      echocardiography. RESULTS: There was good reliability between the two 6MWT 
      (distance; intraclass correlation coefficient 0.95, r = 0.89, Borg; intraclass 
      correlation coefficient 0.85, r = 0.91, both P < 0.00 for r), and Bland Altman 
      plots demonstrate good agreement between measures 1 week apart. Forehead and 
      finger oximetry were more reliable than earlobe (intraclass correlation 
      coefficient 0.64, 0.60, 0.24; r = 0.46, 0.47, 0.14; n = 22, 17, 7, respectively). 
      Forehead desaturation correlated with forced expiratory volume in 1 s (r = 0.55, 
      P = 0.01) and forced vital capacity (r = 0.59, P = 0.01). Distance correlated 
      with all physiological measures: forced expiratory volume in 1 s (r = 0.55, P = 
      0.01), forced vital capacity (r = 0.61, P = 0.01) and diffusing capacity of 
      carbon monoxide (r = 0.42, P = 0.05). Computed tomography extent and patterns of 
      disease correlated poorly with 6MWT measures, and global measures of SSc 
      correlated only with post-test Borg score. CONCLUSIONS: The 6MWT is feasible and 
      reliable in SSc lung disease, but forehead oximetry should be used. The test 
      measurements correlate reasonably but variably with functional and morphological 
      measures of disease severity.
