TI  - Sleep-disordered breathing and continuous positive airway pressure compliance in 
      a group of commercial bus drivers in Hong Kong.
PG  - 723-30
AB  - OBJECTIVE AND BACKGROUND: Sleep-disordered breathing may predispose drivers to 
      road traffic accidents. However, CPAP acceptance and compliance among commercial 
      bus drivers is unknown. METHODS: We conducted a sleep questionnaire survey at a 
      bus depot followed by random selection of bus drivers for home sleep study with 
      the mesam iv device. Bus drivers with a respiratory disturbance index 
      (RDI)>or=5/h were invited to have hospital-based polysomnography for confirmation 
      followed by nasal CPAP treatment. RESULTS: Out of 1477 bus drivers, 1016 (971 
      men) were interviewed with mean (SD) age of 45.3 (7.5) years, BMI 24.9 (3.6) 
      kg/m2, neck circumference 38.9 (3.1) cm and Epworth sleepiness score 4.8 (4.0). 
      Sleepiness at work was reported by 60.9%, snoring>or=3 nights per week 23.9%, 
      witnessed apnoea 3.7% and having fallen asleep during driving by 24%. Among 211 
      who underwent home sleep study, 85 (40.3%), 55 (26.1%) and 37 (17.5%) had 
      RDI>or=5, >or=10 and >or=15/h respectively. BMI, snoring intensity and neck 
      circumference were the positive independent factors associated with the RDI. Of 
      the 25 drivers who attended polysomnography, nine accepted CPAP prescription with 
      improvement of Epworth sleepiness score, trail B and digit span after 3 months 
      with CPAP usage of 4.5 (1.3) h/night. CONCLUSION: The estimated minimum 
      prevalence rates of sleep-disordered breathing and obstructive sleep apnoea 
      syndrome were 8.4% and 5.4% respectively among the bus drivers. Home CPAP 
      acceptance was low among bus drivers with sleep apnoea but there was significant 
      improvement of subjective sleepiness and cognitive function among those on CPAP 
      treatment.
