TI  - Tuberculous peritonitis developing during chemotherapy for pulmonary and 
      intestinal tuberculosis: a case report.
PG  - 257-60
AB  - This report is of a case of tuberculous peritonitis that developed during 
      antituberculous chemotherapy. A 54-year-old man had been diagnosed as all-drug 
      susceptible pulmonary and intestinal tuberculosis, and treatment with isoniazid, 
      ethambutol and rifampicin had been initiated. About 5 months later, while still 
      undergoing therapy, a large amount of ascites developed. A diagnostic laparoscopy 
      was performed but due to the adhesion between the greater omentum and the 
      parietal peritoneum, intestinal perforation occurred. An emergency operation was 
      performed and the diagnosis of tuberculous peritonitis was confirmed. There are 
      few reports of abdominal tuberculosis developing during antituberculous 
      chemotherapy. In this case a paradoxical response may have been involved in the 
      pathogenesis.
