TI  - Antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody positive alveolar haemorrhage during 
      propylthiouracil therapy for hyperthyroidism.
PG  - 532-5
AB  - Recently, propylthiouracil (PTU) has been thought to be one of the possible 
      causes of antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody (ANCA)-associated small vessel 
      vasculitis syndrome, resulting in glomerulonephritis and, infrequently, diffuse 
      alveolar haemorrhage (DAH). The mechanism of ANCA-positive vasculitis during PTU 
      therapy is still unknown. Herein, we describe the case of a 59-year-old woman who 
      developed myeloperoxidase (MPO)- and proteinase 3 (PR3)-ANCA positive DAH, 
      without any other organ system involvement, during PTU therapy. Diminution and 
      discontinuation of PTU resulted in a positive response. To our knowledge, this is 
      the first documentation of both MPO- and PR3-ANCA-positive DAH, without systemic 
      manifestations, developing during PTU therapy.
