TI  - Management of COPD in Asia: A position statement of the Asian Pacific Society of 
      Respirology.
PG  - 1018-1025
LID - 10.1111/resp.13633 [doi]
AB  - Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) is a major disease in Asia. However, 
      how to manage specifically Asian COPD patients has not been proposed. Awareness 
      of COPD is very low and underdiagnosis/undertreatment is common in Asian 
      countries. Low utilization of pulmonary function test and inhalers is also a 
      problem. Moreover, high smoking prevalence and air pollution are barriers to 
      managing Asian patients with COPD. The relatively low body mass index of Asian 
      patients with COPD can increase their risk for experiencing adverse effects from 
      COPD drugs. Physicians should consider the unique features of Asian populations 
      with COPD such as the high prevalence rates of bronchiectasis and 
      tuberculosis-destroyed lungs, biomass smoke exposure and parasitic infection.
