TI  - Congenital cystic adenomatoid malformation of the lung: hazards of delayed 
      diagnosis.
PG  - 1058-60
LID - 10.1111/j.1440-1843.2009.01603.x [doi]
AB  - Congenital cystic adenomatoid malformation is a rare pulmonary developmental 
      anomaly, which typically manifests in neonates and infants. Presentation in 
      adulthood is uncommon, with <60 cases reported in the literature. The majority of 
      cases involve one lobe only. We report a case of type 1 congenital cystic 
      adenomatoid malformation in an adult presenting with a respiratory tract 
      infection and haemoptysis. At thoracotomy, complex cystic masses were noted in 
      the right upper and lower lobes. Lung-sparing surgery, in the form of two 
      segmentectomies and a non-anatomical resection, was performed in order to avoid 
      pneumonectomy. Such presentations may be problematic as potentially incomplete 
      resections may increase the risk of complications and malignant transformation. 
      This suggests the importance of appropriate clinical and radiological follow up.
