DP  - 2008 Jun
TI  - Periodic breathing and dysphagia associated with a localized lateral medullary 
      infarction.
PG  - 608-10
LID - 10.1111/j.1440-1843.2008.01267.x [doi]
AB  - A 77-year-old man experienced sudden onset of dysphagia. An MRI revealed a left 
      lateral medullary infarction. Portable polysomnography showed Cheyne-Stokes-like 
      breathing with marked desaturation (lowest SaO(2) 74%) associated with apnoea. 
      Oxygen administration reduced the apnoea-hypopnoea index from 30.1 to 20.0 per 
      hour. Sustained periodic breathing under normoxaemia suggested that the brainstem 
      lesion, rather than peripheral factors such as prolonged lung-chemoreceptor 
      circulatory delay, was responsible for the abnormal breathing. Polysomnography or 
      overnight SaO(2) monitoring should be considered for patients with dysphagia due 
      to a brainstem lesion, to investigate the possible presence of abnormal 
      breathing.
