VI  - 13
IP  - 4
DP  - 2008 Jun
TI  - Neutrophil elastase activity in acute lung injury and respiratory distress 
      syndrome.
PG  - 581-4
LID - 10.1111/j.1440-1843.2008.01283.x [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND AND OBJECTIVE: Neutrophil elastase (NE) may play a key role in the 
      development of acute lung injury (ALI) or ARDS. NE activity (NEA) was measured in 
      patients with ALI treated with a selective NE inhibitor. METHODS: NEA and 
      NE-alpha1-antitrypsin (NE-AT) complex were measured in plasma before, during and 
      after the administration of the selective NE inhibitor, sivelestat, in 32 
      patients with a diagnosis of ALI or ARDS. NEA index (NEAI) was calculated as 
      NEA/NE-AT. The sequential organ failure assessment (SOFA) score and the ratio 
      PaO(2)/fraction of inspired oxygen (FiO(2)) were measured. RESULTS: NEA and NE-AT 
      was raised in all patients. Sivelestat reduced NEAI and NEA (P < 0.01 for both) 
      but not NE-AT and NEA, and NEAI returned to pretreatment levels. NEA correlated 
      closely with NE-AT before, but not after treatment. No relationship was observed 
      between these indices and SOFA score or PaO(2)/FiO(2) ratio. CONCLUSIONS: 
      Sivelestat reduced NEA and NEAI in patients with ALI or ARDS suggesting NE 
      inhibition. A larger study is needed to determine the relationship of NEA, NE-AT 
      and NEAI with the outcome of ALI/ARDS.
