TI  - Endobronchial ultrasound for detection of tracheomalacia from chronic compression 
      by vascular ring.
PG  - 299-301
AB  - A 67-year-old female chronic smoker was evaluated for an asymptomatic right 
      paratracheal mass and the diagnosis of double-arch aorta was made. She returned 2 
      years later with dyspnoea on exertion, productive cough and wheeze on lying 
      supine. Flow volume curve showed variable intrathoracic airway obstruction, and 
      bronchoscopy revealed extrinsic compression of the trachea by double-arch aorta 
      with destruction of the cartilaginous layer visualized on endobronchial 
      ultrasonography. Endobronchial ultrasonography may be a useful adjunctive tool 
      for the identification of adults at risk of postoperative tracheomalacia where 
      tracheopexy or airway stenting can be performed concurrently or sequentially if 
      surgery is contemplated.
