TI  - Percentage reduction of pleural effusion as a simple predictor of pleural 
      scarring in tuberculous pleuritis.
PG  - 515-9
AB  - OBJECTIVE: The aim of the present study was to evaluate the utility of serum and 
      pleural fluid biomarkers for predicting residual pleural scarring (RPS) in 
      tuberculous pleuritis. METHODOLOGY: A retrospective study of patients with 
      pleural tuberculosis was performed. Demographic data, clinical parameters, 
      haematological indices, serum and pleural fluid biochemistry and pleural effusion 
      area were assessed for correlation with the extent of RPS. RESULTS: RPS was found 
      in 41.4% of the 70 cases evaluated, with significant pleural scarring being 
      present in 7.1%. It was more common in males (odds ratio 5.55). Among the 
      variables studied, only the percentage reduction of the effusion after 2 weeks of 
      treatment was found to independently predict the extent of RPS (r=-0.502, 
      P<0.001). CONCLUSION: RPS was more common in males and the percentage reduction 
      in pleural effusion on CXR after 2 weeks of treatment was found to be a useful 
      predictor of RPS.
