IS  - 1323-7799 (Linking)
VI  - 11 Suppl
DP  - 2006 Jan
TI  - Monitoring markers of disease activity for interstitial lung diseases with serum 
      surfactant proteins A and D.
PG  - S51-4
AB  - OBJECTIVES: Surfactant protein (SP) A and D are specific serum markers for 
      interstitial lung diseases including idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (IPF). The 
      authors evaluated the critical roles of these markers on the prognoses of 
      patients with IPF and the mechanisms of their elevation in sera. METHODOLOGY: The 
      authors evaluated the relationship between prognosis and the serum markers in 82 
      IPF patients. The protein content and mRNA expression of the markers were 
      evaluated using rats with interstitial pneumonia induced by bleomycin 
      administration. RESULTS: Higher levels of serum SP-D at the time of the initial 
      visit to the Sapporo Medical University Hospital were associated with poorer 
      prognoses, while SP-A showed no significant affect on survival. Causes of the 
      elevation in sera were due to the acceleration of, not only production in the 
      lungs, leakage into the circulation. The elevation was associated with alveolitis 
      but not fibrosis. CONCLUSIONS: SP-D is a good predictor of the prognosis in 
      patients with IPF.
