DP  - 2005 Nov
TI  - Chronic pulmonary scedosporiosis simulating aspergillosis.
PG  - 682-4
AB  - Scedosporium apiospermum, a ubiquitously distributed saprophyte, is emerging as 
      an important pathogen in immunocompromised patients. We describe a 72-year-old 
      patient with chronic S. apiospermum infection of the lung simulating 
      aspergilloma. His medical history was unremarkable except that he had undergone 
      partial lung resection as a treatment for pulmonary tuberculosis several decades 
      previously. He had no underlying immunosuppressive conditions. This patient 
      illustrates that pulmonary scedosporiosis is not confined to immunocompromised 
      patients and that the clinical presentation may be indistinguishable from that of 
      aspergilloma.
