TI  - Pancoast's syndrome in a patient with B-cell lymphoma diagnosed and confirmed 
      with immunoglobulin gene rearrangement.
PG  - 137-40
AB  - Pancoast's syndrome due to malignant lymphoma is extremely rare. A case of 
      diffuse large B-cell lymphoma presenting as Pancoast's syndrome is described. A 
      66-year-old man complained of pain and weakness of the right arm, and CXR 
      revealed a right apical lung tumour. Histological findings were consistent with 
      it being a diffuse large cell type lymphoma and Southern blot analysis revealed 
      clonal rearrangement of the immunoglobulin heavy-chain JH. Thus, the tumour in 
      this patient was diagnosed to be diffuse large B-cell lymphoma. Malignant 
      lymphoma is an extremely rare cause of Pancoast's syndrome and only five cases 
      have been described. This is the first reported case of Pancoast's syndrome 
      caused by B-cell lymphoma, which was accurately diagnosed by analysis of gene 
      rearrangement.
