DP  - 2003 Mar
TI  - A case of hypersensitivity pneumonitis caused by Humicola fuscoatra.
PG  - 95-8
AB  - A 51-year-old housewife with hypersensitivity pneumonitis caused by Humicola 
      fuscoatra is reported. The diagnosis was made by an inhalation challenge with H. 
      fuscoatra antigen. She was admitted for diagnosis and treatment of a fever and 
      productive cough. Auscultation of her lungs revealed inspiratory fine crackles. 
      Her chest CT showed diffuse miliary nodules in a centri-lobular distribution with 
      patchy ground glass opacities. Findings of transbronchial lung biopsy and BAL 
      fluid were compatible with a hypersensitivity pneumonitis. Her symptoms worsened 
      on returning home, which suggested the existence of some aetiological agent in 
      the subject's house. H. fuscoatra, Penicillium decumbens and Aspergillus 
      versicolor were isolated from a number of rooms. High titres of serum anti H. 
      fuscoatra, P. decumbens and A. versicolor were detected. Inhalation challenge 
      tests with both P. decumbens and A. versicolor antigen were negative, in contrast 
      to that with H. fuscoatra which was positive. Based on these results, we advised 
      the patient to cleanse her entire house. Since cleaning, her symptoms have not 
      worsened upon returning home. This is the first report of hypersensitivity 
      pneumonitis caused by H. fuscoatra antigen.
